On May 17, 2:30 pm, Henry J Cobb <h...@io.com> wrote:
>
> Finally allow the backstab bonus against any unaware foe.  So if they
> are fleeing, asleep, peaceful (or tame), or just made a confused,
> stunned or displacement (or invisibility) distracted strike against the
> wrong target or empty space then they are open for a backstab.

As a first pass, I tried patching it so that you can backstab if the
monser thinks you are in a different location to your actual one (ie
mtmp->mux != u.ux || mtmp->muy != u.uy). This handles both
invisibility and displacement sensibly, but unfortunately, these
values are only recomputed when the monster moves, not when the player
moves. So if you are fast, you can get a lot of backstabs in without
needing to be hidden, which is overpowered and isn't very realistic.

Perhaps I could put an extra call to set_apparxy() in the appropriate
spot in uhitm.c. Hmm.

I imagine that the Stealth intrinsic should also factor into this
somehow. What is the practical application of stealth anyhow?

Perhaps it would be reasonable to allow backstabbing to any character
with stealth, rather than restricting it to Rogues. Rogues would have
it from the outset, being naturally Stealthy, while other classes
would have to work somehow to achieve it, trading it off against other
intrinsics they might desire.

